MCP gateway — closing the ambient-credential side door
=======================================================
Date    : 2026-07-28
Cluster : kind-platform-agent (local, $0)
Origin  : docs/reference/gcp-agentic-ai-architecture-center.md (shared-MCP gap)


What was open
--------------
Phase 1a removed the ambient-kubeconfig path from the executor. Its docstring is
explicit: "The ambient-context path is GONE on purpose (Phase 1a) ... blast
radius was whatever that context could reach and a routing bug failed open."

The MCP gateway kept exactly that path. Every tool shelled out to a bare
`kubectl`:

    cmd = ["kubectl", "get", resource, "--namespace", namespace, ...]
    subprocess.run(["kubectl", "apply", "-f", "-", "--namespace", namespace], ...)

No `--kubeconfig`, no namespace bound, and `kubectl_apply` accepts an **arbitrary
manifest into an arbitrary namespace**. The front door was bolted; the side door
was not. `grep tenant src/agents/ai/gateway/mcp_server.py` returned nothing — the
kill-switch is "disable this tool for everyone", never "for this tenant".

The GCP multi-tenant guide names this directly: a *shared* MCP deployment
requires propagating identity to the server, or the fine-grained access control
it is supposed to enforce does not exist.


What changed
-------------
- `_kubectl()` builds every argv, pinning `--kubeconfig` to the scope in force.
  Request-scoped via `ContextVar` rather than a tool parameter, because handlers
  dispatch as `fn(**arguments)` from the advertised MCP schema — an extra
  argument would let a *caller* name its own credential, which is the opposite
  of the point.
- Mutating cluster tools **fail closed** with no scope. Reads stay available
  unscoped, because the verified anonymous kagent round trip goes through them,
  and are pinned whenever a scope is present.
- The namespace check is `guard_scoped_action` — the same function the three
  runners call, not a lookalike of it.


Live behaviour (kind, real SA token)
-------------------------------------
    A. scoped read, own namespace (acme-dev-logging)
       success: True | deployment.apps/acme-dev-logging-loki-gateway

    B. same gateway -> neighbour tenant (globex-dev-observability)
       success: False
       namespace 'globex-dev-observability' is outside the scope of tenant 'acme'

    C. mutation with no scope at all (apply a Namespace named "pwned")
       success: False
       refusing 'kubectl_apply' without a scoped credential — this gateway no
       longer inherits the ambient kubeconfig

    D. in-scope namespace, but a resource the Role does not grant
       success: False
       Error from server (Forbidden): secrets is forbidden: User
       "system:serviceaccount:acme-dev-logging:platform-agent" ...

D is the one worth keeping. B is our guard saying no; **D is the API server
saying no** — inside a namespace our own check allowed. The credential is the
boundary and our gate is the early, attributable refusal in front of it.


Falsification
--------------
The pinning test is parametrised over all five cluster tools rather than
spot-checking one. Reintroducing a bare `kubectl` in a single tool
(`kubectl_logs`) failed exactly that tool and nothing else:

    FAILED test_every_cluster_tool_is_pinned_to_the_credential[kubectl_logs-args1]
    1 failed, 11 passed        (restored -> 12 passed)

`test_no_cluster_tool_escapes_the_gate` compares the catalog's `kubectl_*` tools
against `CLUSTER_TOOLS` by equality, so adding a sixth kubectl tool without
declaring it breaks the gate loudly instead of silently reopening the door.


WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO
----------------------
- **Unscoped reads are still ambient.** `kubectl_get`/`logs`/`describe` with no
  scope run against whatever context is current, exactly as before. That is a
  deliberate carve-out to keep the verified anonymous path working, not an
  argument that reads are harmless — a read of another tenant's logs is a
  disclosure. Closing it means giving the kagent path a scope first.
- **No tenant rate limiting.** Quotas stop at Capsule's cpu/memory/pods; nothing
  counts model calls or tool invocations per tenant. Still open in `NEXT_PLAN`.
- The gateway does not verify that a scope it was handed matches the caller —
  it trusts its constructor. Minting remains the broker's job.
